# SYNOPSIS

```text
size[4] Twalk tag[2] fid[4] newfid[4] nwname[2] nwname*(wname[s])
size[4] Rwalk tag[2] nwqid[2] nwqid*(qid[13])
```

# DESCRIPTION

`Twalk` asks the server to make <newfid> refer to the file reached by walking from <fid>'s file through the names <wname>, one after another.
<newfid> must not be in use, unless it is <fid> itself, and it may not be `NOFID` (`0xffffffff`).
<fid> must be known on the connection and must not be open: a fid that `Topen` or `Tcreate` has opened (see open(5)) cannot walk.
The message is 9P2000's, type 110 and 111, unchanged, and needs no extension.

The names are walked in order.
Each step needs the file reached so far to be a directory the request's user may search; the server decides that, and fsd(4) checks the directory's `x` bit.
If the first name cannot be walked, the reply is `Rerror`.
Otherwise the reply is `Rwalk`, with the qid of each file reached, in order: <nwqid> is <nwname> if every name was walked, or the index of the first that failed.
Only when every name is walked is <newfid> made, or <fid> moved when <newfid> is <fid>; otherwise neither changes, as the reply's short <nwqid> says.
A walk of no names makes <newfid> another fid for <fid>'s file, and succeeds.

The name `..` is the parent directory.
`..` at the root the fid was attached at (see attach(5)) is that root, so no walk leaves the tree its attach chose; the framework, not the file server, keeps this, and every fid a walk makes remembers its attach's root.
The name `.` is not used in the protocol: a walk of `.` is refused, as is an empty name, a name holding `/`, a name that is not valid UTF-8 and a name holding a control character (ADR-0013).
The framework refuses these before the file server sees them.

A message carries at most 16 names or qids, `P9_MAXWELEM`; a `Twalk` or `Rwalk` with more does not decode, and the server hangs up rather than answering one.
A path is not limited, only the names one message carries: vx-9p's client walks a longer path in messages of 16, each later one from the fid the one before made.
The client counts a short `Rwalk` as `file does not exist`, and clunks the fid an earlier message of the same path made.

A connection holds at most 256 fids, `P9_MAX_FIDS`.

